                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_system:        `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> The plant doesn't lack capacity — it amplifies normal volatility because a static plan and reactive ordering feed the bullwhip. Better information and buffer discipline damp the loop. Now every spend you make should attack the cause, not chase the last headline.`,
                    coach_r1_capacity:      `<strong>'Just add a line' is the recency trap.</strong> Your average utilization is 78% — you don't lack capacity, you lack the ability to flex to a one-month peak and the discipline to stop over-ordering. Buy a permanent line and you'll carry fixed cost through eleven slow months for a problem capacity didn't cause.`,
                    coach_r1_supplier:      `<strong>The resin supplier is a real risk — but not the whole story.</strong> Even with perfect supply, a static plan and reactive ordering would still whipsaw your inventory. Don't let the visible vendor problem hide the self-inflicted loop underneath.`,
                    coach_r1_badluck:       `<strong>'Bad luck, ride it out' is exactly how this repeats.</strong> The pineapple season and the competitor outage were external — but doubling production afterwards was a decision. Call that 'luck' and next peak you make the same self-inflicted glut.`,
                    coach_r1_noloop:        `<strong>If you see no loop, you'll keep feeding it.</strong> The stockout led you to over-order, which became the glut, which will lead to cuts, which sets up the next stockout. Naming the over-react / over-correct loop is the first step to damping it.`,
                    coach_r1_underattribute: `<strong>You're under-counting your own role.</strong> Yes, demand swings ±40% externally — but the $3.1M glut came from doubling orders, not from the weather. Until you own the self-inflicted share, you'll keep treating symptoms.`,
                    coach_r2_signnow:       `<strong>Growth before stability is Common Error number three.</strong> You signed a demanding $14M anchor client on a plant that already misses 96% at 81% OTD. If you can't serve today's volatility, adding peak-season pressure turns one penalty clause into three. What had to be true operationally before you signed?`,
                    coach_r2_decline:       `<strong>Declining is safe, but it leaves $14M on the table.</strong> The lesson isn't 'never grow' — it's 'stabilize first, then grow.' A conditional yes that fixes service and sources the +20% the right way captures the contract without betting the plant.`,
                    coach_r2_conditional:   `<strong>A conditional yes is the disciplined call.</strong> You take the $14M only once service is stabilized and the +20% is sourced sensibly. Growth follows operational stability rather than preceding it — now make the capacity mix match.`,
                    coach_r2_permanent:     `<strong>You committed ~$4M to a permanent third line so you'll 'never miss a peak again'.</strong> But average utilization was already 78% and the peak was one weather-driven month. Now you carry fixed cost across eleven slow months and your utilization drops further. Did you solve a volatility problem, or buy expensive insurance against last month's headline?`,
                    coach_r2_flexible:      `<strong>Leaning on flexible capacity is the right read of the distribution.</strong> Overtime, temp lines and contract manufacturing absorb the one-month peak without saddling eleven slow months with fixed cost. You meet peak service and keep utilization healthy.`,
                    coach_r2_point:         `<strong>Planning to a point forecast is what got you here.</strong> Demand is a distribution that swings ±40% — staff to a single number and you're wrong almost every month. Size to a service level instead.`,
                    coach_r2_peak:          `<strong>Sizing to the absolute peak is the mirror image of the stockout panic.</strong> You'll never miss — and you'll carry idle capacity and cost all year for a peak that lasts a month. Pick a service level on the distribution, not the worst case.`,
                    coach_r3_information:   `<strong>Tilting the budget to information is the Industry 4.0 insight.</strong> Demand-sensing/S&OP and a supplier control tower attack the bullwhip and the resin blind spot — the binding constraints. You're spending where the decision quality is, not where the speed is.`,
                    coach_r3_throughput:    `<strong>You put the digital budget into a faster warehouse.</strong> Throughput is up — but your problem was never raw speed; it's that you plan on a spreadsheet and can't see demand or the resin delay coming. You automated the part that was already adequate. Which investment would have let you decide better, not just run faster?`,
                    coach_r3_mixed:         `<strong>A spread is defensible — but tilt it toward information.</strong> For a volatility-driven problem, the signal and the decision beat raw throughput. The more weight on demand-sensing and the control tower, the more you damp the bullwhip and see the supply risk coming.`,
                    coach_r3_noprocess:     `<strong>Tools without a new process change nothing.</strong> Install demand-sensing and then keep planning on the monthly spreadsheet, and you've bought dashboards, not decisions. The value is the S&OP cadence the technology enables — not the technology.`,
                    coach_r4_statistical:   `<strong>Statistical, item-differentiated safety stock is the right redesign.</strong> Your ±40% export items get the protection they need while the steady QSR items stop tying up working capital. That's how you move toward 30 days without cratering service.`,
                    coach_r4_flat:          `<strong>One flat safety-stock number is the static policy that started this.</strong> It over-stocks the stable items and under-protects the volatile ones — the worst of both. Size safety stock to each item's demand variability and lead-time risk.`,
                    coach_r4_cut:           `<strong>Slashing all safety stock hits 30 days and then misses every volatile order.</strong> Inventory isn't the enemy — undisciplined, undifferentiated inventory is. Cut the steady items hard, protect the ±40% items, and service holds.`,
                    coach_r4_stockpile:     `<strong>A giant resin stockpile 'solves' supply by recreating the glut.</strong> You've swapped a finished-goods pile for a raw-material one and tied up the working capital you just freed. Dual-source and size a real strategic buffer instead of hoarding.`,
                    coach_r4_ignoreresin:   `<strong>Your finished-goods buffer is optimized beautifully — and your resin is still single-sourced from Asia on a 6-week lead time with a delay already flagged.</strong> One disruption upstream idles the whole optimized plant. Why did you manage the visible buffer and underweight the supply risk?`,
                    coach_r4_otdonly:       `<strong>Managing to OTD alone recreates the glut.</strong> Chase service with no eye on inventory cost and you'll over-stock your way back to 52 days. The scorecard has to hold service AND cost AND utilization together.`,
                    coach_r4_costonly:      `<strong>Cost-per-unit alone is how service slips back to 81%.</strong> Squeeze cost without watching OTD and the penalties and churn return. Balance the scorecard, don't optimize one number.`,
                    coach_r4_overconfident: `<strong>That's a lot of conviction for a plan that still amplifies the loop.</strong> You haven't damped the bullwhip — a repeat +38% peak would whipsaw you again. Confidence isn't a plan; show me the loop is broken.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That's the integrated plan.</strong> Statistical buffers, a dual-sourced resin with a sized strategic buffer, and a balanced scorecard — capacity, information and buffers working together. A repeat +38% peak now gets absorbed instead of amplified. That's the system stabilized.`,

